THE REPOSITORY IS MIT WITH ONE EXCEPTION, AND THE EXCEPTION IS A DIRECTORY.

Every file is available under the terms below, with no copyleft strings, except
for `AtlasTool/ten/`. That one directory is derived from TombEngine and carries
TombEngine's own licence: Modified MIT, FOR NON-COMMERCIAL USE ONLY. Its terms
are in `AtlasTool/ten/LICENSE` and what is in it is described in
`AtlasTool/ten/README.md`.

The boundary is physical so that it can be checked by looking at a path. Nothing
outside that directory depends on it, and the Room View preview it serves is the
only feature that loads it. `AtlasTool/js/engine.js`, `js/shaders.js` and
`js/presets.js` — the shared export pipeline — do not import from it, which is
asserted by `AtlasTool/tools/validate-licensing.mjs`.

The directory is SEPARABLE, and that is asserted rather than claimed: delete it and
the tool still slices atlases and generates and exports every material map, losing
only two previews. Nothing in it generates a map or touches the export path.

What is left outside it is one scalar, one prose citation and some development
tooling that is never distributed. All of it is listed in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.

Until 2026-09-13 the project shipped under GPL-3.0, because two seamless-tiling
shaders (`seamlessMaker` and `seamlessSplat`) had been ported from Materialize
(BoundingBoxSoftware, GPL-3.0). Both were removed — from the live tool and from
the archived v1 copy — and replaced with independent implementations of published
techniques:

  * multi-band (Laplacian pyramid) edge blending — Burt & Adelson 1983; GPU
    formulation per "GPU-Friendly Laplacian Texture Blending", JCGT 14(1), 2025
  * variance-preserving ("histogram-preserving") stamp blending — Heitz & Neyret,
    High-Performance Graphics 2018

No Materialize code remains anywhere in this repository, including in
`Archive/TextureTool-v1/`. The archived v1 tool was ported to the same MIT
replacements rather than being left GPL-3.0; its README records that it is
therefore not a byte-for-byte historical copy, and points at git for the
unmodified original.

Third-party components bundled or loaded at runtime keep their own licenses; see
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md. All of them are permissive (MIT / Apache-2.0 / BSD).

